# child_repo_10_LOC (Level 2) - example environment file
#
# A TEMPLATE, not a live configuration. It is tracked in version control and it
# documents every environment variable the Level 2 /health server reads. There
# are exactly two, both listed at the end of this file with the defaults this
# repository ships.
#
# To use it locally, copy it and edit the copy - never this file:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a     # export the values into your shell
#     python server.py
#     curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
#
# `python server.py` writes exactly one line and then stays alive, naming the
# address it actually bound and the application serving it:
#
#     listening on http://0.0.0.0:8000/health (child_repo_10_LOC 1.0.0)
#
# Your `.env` copy is a local file and must never be committed. Nothing here is
# parsed at run time: no dotenv package is installed, because this composition
# carries zero third-party dependencies and reading a two-line file is not a
# reason to acquire its first one. `health.py` reads `os.environ` directly, so a
# variable takes effect only once something has exported it - which also makes
# an inline setting the shortest way to try one:
#
#     HEALTH_PORT=8100 HEALTH_HOST=127.0.0.1 python server.py
#
# Resolution precedence for HEALTH_PORT and HEALTH_HOST, highest first - the
# same chain at all three tiers of this composition:
#
#     environment variable  ->  config/health.json  ->  compiled-in literal
#
# The literal fallback is required, not optional: it guarantees /health still
# serves a valid response when `config/health.json` is missing - from a
# container image, say - which is precisely the failure a health endpoint has
# to survive. An endpoint that cannot answer because its own configuration is
# absent is worse than no endpoint at all. The literals in `health.py` are the
# same 8000 and 0.0.0.0 shown below, so a missing configuration file degrades
# to identical behaviour rather than to a failure.
#
# The chain degrades one link at a time, which makes "set but unusable"
# different from "unset": `HEALTH_PORT=` and `HEALTH_PORT=not-a-number` both
# fall through to `config/health.json` first, and only then to the literal. The
# process starts either way - a typo in the environment can never stop this
# endpoint answering. `HEALTH_PORT=0` is honoured rather than refused and means
# "let the operating system choose a free port", which is how a test binds a
# listener without colliding with a server already on 8000; the number the
# kernel chose is the one reported in the start-up line.
#
# That chain governs these two listener values and nothing else. Every other
# member of the response is resolved with no environment step at all: `name`
# and `version` are read from `pyproject.toml`, and the resource path and the
# `UP` status are frozen contract constants compiled into `health.py`. The
# section below says what that means for each of them, and why.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately NOT overridable here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * `name` and `version` - the first two members of the /health response body -
#   are NOT environment-overridable at this tier. They are read once, at import,
#   from `pyproject.toml`, this tier's single identity manifest, which declares
#   name `child_repo_10_LOC` and version `1.0.0`. Identity is declared by the
#   repository rather than injected by whoever starts the process, so do not
#   introduce APP_NAME or APP_VERSION variables: they are not part of the design
#   and nothing reads them.
#
# * The resource path `/health` and the literal `status` value `UP` are frozen
#   contract constants, compiled into `health.py`. They are not
#   environment-overridable, and they are not settings at all:
#   `config/health.json` restates both so that an operator can see the whole
#   shape of what is served in one place, but a value there that differs from
#   the frozen literal is rejected - the endpoint keeps serving `/health` and
#   `UP`, and `server.py` prints one warning line naming the rejected value.
#   Every workflow assertion and container HEALTHCHECK in this composition is
#   written against those two literals, so a settable path would move the
#   endpoint away from where monitoring looks for it, and a settable status
#   would let a deployment misreport its own health. There is no HEALTH_PATH
#   variable and no HEALTH_STATUS variable, and neither may be added.
#
# * This tier prefixes both of its variable names, as shown below. The apex
#   application of this composition uses the bare `HOST` and `PORT` for its own
#   listener, and nothing in this repository reads either of them - setting them
#   here has no effect whatsoever. That asymmetry is deliberate: this tier owns
#   a broader process environment, so a prefix keeps the health listener's
#   settings unambiguous. It must not be "harmonized".
#
# * No further variable belongs in this file. Documenting one would advertise a
#   setting that no code reads: this application has no data store, no user
#   sign-in, no transport-layer encryption to terminate and no logging
#   framework. Every value below is a plain, non-sensitive default, and it stays
#   that way - because this file is committed, nothing private may ever be
#   written into it.
#
# The normative definition of the endpoint and of these values is
# `docs/health-endpoint.md`, in the apex repository of this composition.
# =============================================================================

# TCP port the /health listener accepts connections on. Default: 8000, this
# tier's reserved port in the composition, so this application can run beside
# its siblings on one host without a collision. Change it locally if 8000 is
# already taken - in your `.env` copy, or inline on the command line.
HEALTH_PORT=8000

# Address the /health listener binds to; validated, then handed unchanged to the
# `ThreadingHTTPServer` constructor. Default: 0.0.0.0, meaning every local
# interface, so a published container port stays reachable from outside the host
# that binds it. Set it to 127.0.0.1 to confine the listener to the loopback
# interface during local development. An IPv6 literal is accepted too - `::1`
# for loopback alone, `::` for every interface - and the address family is
# selected from the literal itself, so no second setting is needed.
#
# Note that 0.0.0.0 is a BIND address only and is never a destination: a probe
# always addresses http://127.0.0.1:<HEALTH_PORT>/health, because a health probe
# asserts the state of *this* process.
HEALTH_HOST=0.0.0.0
